[
    {
        "content": "<p>Answering <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"479299\">@Jovan Gerbscheid</span>'s question from <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"428973\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/428973-nightly-testing/topic/nightly.23215.20adaptations.20for.20nightly-2026-05-03/with/593315051\">#nightly-testing &gt; nightly#215 adaptations for nightly-2026-05-03</a> <br>\n<span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"479299\">Jovan Gerbscheid</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/428973-nightly-testing/topic/nightly.23215.20adaptations.20for.20nightly-2026-05-03/near/593313438\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Ok, so I've run into an issue: when adding <code>@[to_app]</code> to <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.mapComp%27_inv_whiskerRight_mapComp%27%E2%82%80%E2%82%82%E2%82%83_inv#doc\">docs#CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.mapComp'_inv_whiskerRight_mapComp'₀₂₃_inv</a>,</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">to_app</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mapComp'_inv_whiskerRight_mapComp'₀₂₃_inv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₀₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≫</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₂₃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mapComp'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₀₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₁₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₀₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h₀₂</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">▷</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₂₃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≫</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mapComp'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₀₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₂₃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≫</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₀₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">◁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mapComp'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₁₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₂₃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₁₃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h₁₃</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≫</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mapComp'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₀₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f₁₃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inv</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The <code>Bicategory.associator</code>, <code>α_</code>, (which is \"equal\" to the identity morphism) currently disappears in the <code>to_app</code> version. It seems a bit inconsistent that we abuse definitonal equality in the <code>app</code> version, but not in the original. <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"286014\">Robin Carlier</span> do you know if this is intentional and what the reasoning for this is?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I would say this is intentional in the following sense: when functors are bundled (i.e not at the to_app) we want to keep the associators but want to make them go away as soon as you apply a field projection. I think <code>to_app</code> is running a simp only that simplifies the <code>app</code> fields of unitors and associators and rewrites <code>comp_id</code> and <code>id_comp</code> </p>\n<p>This is part of the de facto philosophy of that part of the library which is \"you want <code>(F ⋙ G) ⋙ H = F ⋙ (G ⋙ H)</code> to be as opaque as possible (e.g., because you want to put different instances on them) but you want <code>(F ⋙ G).obj X = G.obj (F.obj X)</code> to be as transparent as possible (because it makes rewriting easier) (edit: and makes less tedious mathematically-empty terms to insert in practice)\", which is kind of why the <code>@[reduce_proj]</code> suggestion by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110087\">@Kim Morrison</span> in <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"113488\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/backward.2EisDefEq.2ErespectTransparency/with/592600259\">#general &gt; backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency</a>  was quite interesting because it seemed to have exactly that effect of having \"opaque\" bundled objects that become very transparent as soon as you apply a projection.</p>",
        "id": 593317217,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Carlier",
        "timestamp": 1778088363
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Aha interesting. Do you think this is a good argument to prefer the <code>reduce_proj</code> approach over the approach of splitting the transparency classes?</p>",
        "id": 593318421,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1778088737
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I have the gut feeling that this would fit more our use case, yes, but of course I don’t know of all the ramifications!<br>\nWe have several potential other situations like this: <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CategoryTheory.Functor.id#doc\">docs#CategoryTheory.Functor.id</a>, and perhaps <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CategoryTheory.Functor.const#doc\">docs#CategoryTheory.Functor.const</a> are repeated offenders of \"the projections are not transparent enough but we might not want to make the bundled object too reducible\".</p>",
        "id": 593320140,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Carlier",
        "timestamp": 1778089257
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I made <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39867\">#39867</a>, <code>to_dual</code> for <code>Adjunction</code>. Some proofs involving <code>Adjunction.left_triangle</code>/<code>Adjunction.right_triangle</code> fail in CI. Looking more closely at this lemma,</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Adjunction</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">left_triangle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u₁</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Category</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u₂</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Category</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⥤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⥤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">adj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊣</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">whiskerRight</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">adj</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">unit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≫</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">whiskerLeft</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">adj</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">counit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">𝟙</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">𝟭</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⋙</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>it turns out that the composition <code>whiskerRight adj.unit F ≫ F.whiskerLeft adj.counit</code> only makes sense if <code>𝟭 C ⋙ F</code> is the same as <code>F ⋙ 𝟭 D</code>. Note that these are NOT wrapped in a projection <code>.obj</code>.</p>\n<p>What do we want to do about this? Is this the design we want to keep, relying on this defeq abuse? What is especially curious is that there is an explicit argument <code>𝟭 C ⋙ F</code>, which should probably be made to be <code>F</code>, because in explicit arguments we won't be able to rely on <code>implicit_reducible</code>.</p>",
        "id": 597924260,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1779822613
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think we should fix the composition by inserting left/right unitors.</p>",
        "id": 597952307,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1779832950
    }
]